course: dictionary
lesson: 14138
title: Misapprehend: understanding wrongly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Misapprehend is a formal word for misunderstand. It means to understand something wrongly, so the idea in your head does not match what is really true. You will find it in careful writing and in exam questions.
explain: «Sam misapprehended the sign and walked into the wrong classroom.» The clue is walked into the wrong classroom: he got the sign's meaning wrong. Try swapping in a simpler word: «Sam misunderstood the sign and walked into the wrong classroom» still makes sense, so misapprehended means misunderstood.
explain: The noun misapprehension is more common. «She was under the misapprehension that the library was closed on Friday.» Under a misapprehension means you wrongly believe something. In everyday talk, people say «get the wrong idea» instead.
fact: Misapprehend is a verb: misapprehend, misapprehends, misapprehended, misapprehending.
fact: Misapprehend means to understand something wrongly.
fact: Misunderstand is the everyday word with nearly the same meaning.
fact: «Misapprehension» is the noun: a wrong idea or belief, as in «under a misapprehension».
fact: «Misapprehend the situation» means to get the facts of a situation wrong.
exercise: «Ben misapprehended the instructions and built the model upside down.» What does misapprehended mean? | *Understood them wrongly | Forgot them completely | Followed them perfectly | Read them aloud
exercise: «Under the misapprehension that the shop was open, Mia walked all the way there.» What does misapprehension mean here? | A fast train | A loud song | *A wrong belief | A kind gift
exercise: Which everyday word means nearly the same as misapprehend? | Celebrate | Whisper | *Misunderstand | Remember
test: «Lily misapprehended the message and arrived a day late.» What does misapprehended mean? | Lost the message | Sent it back | Read it carefully and understood it | *Understood it wrongly
test: Can you misapprehend a sentence in a book? | No, books cannot be understood | *Yes, if you get its meaning wrong | Only if the book is very long | Only in maths lessons
exam: «Tom was under a misapprehension about the time of the match, so he arrived after it had started.» What does misapprehension mean? | A loud noise | A correct plan | *A wrong belief | A long wait
